      <h1>Lithium Pack Assembly and QC — The Checks That Prevent Field Failures</h1>
      <div class="takeaways"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Most pack failures are assembly failures, not cell failures: mismatched cells, weak welds, high-resistance joints, damaged insulation or a missed leak. QC must test the joints, the isolation and the completed pack — not just the cells.</div>

<h2>Cell matching before assembly</h2>
<p>Cells in a series string should be matched on capacity, internal resistance and self-discharge. A single low-capacity cell limits the whole string and will be driven to the protection threshold first. Ask for the matching criteria and the spread, not just the average.</p>
<h2>The joints are the weak point</h2>
<p>Laser or ultrasonic welds, busbars and bolted terminals all add resistance. A high-resistance joint heats under load, ages faster and can fail open. Check weld pull strength on a sample, measure joint resistance, and specify a torque value with a calibrated tool for bolted connections.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Method</th><th>What it catches</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Cell matching</td><td>Capacity + IR + self-discharge</td><td>Weak cell in a string</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weld quality</td><td>Pull test, cross-section, resistance</td><td>Cold weld, burn-through</td></tr>
<tr><td>Torque</td><td>Calibrated torque wrench, mark</td><td>Loose terminal, heat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insulation</td><td>Megohmmeter at specified voltage</td><td>Creepage, damaged sleeve</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leak</td><td>Pressure/ helium or bubble test</td><td>Seal or vent defect</td></tr>
<tr><td>End-of-line</td><td>Capacity, IR, self-discharge</td><td>Assembly-induced faults</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Insulation and isolation</h2>
<p>A pack is a high-voltage system even at 48 V. Test insulation resistance between live parts and the enclosure, and between the positive and negative terminals, at the specified voltage. A damaged cell sleeve or a pinched sense wire may only show up under vibration — add a vibration check for transport-critical packs.</p>
<h2>Traceability</h2>
<p>Record the cell lot, the weld parameters, the torque values, the operator and the end-of-line result against the pack serial number. Without that record, a field failure cannot be traced to a cause, and a warranty claim becomes an argument.</p>
<h2>What to audit in a supplier</h2>
<ol><li>Is there a documented cell-matching procedure with acceptance limits?</li><li>Are weld parameters recorded per pack, and is pull testing done on a sample?</li><li>Is torque calibrated and marked?</li><li>Is insulation and leak testing performed on 100% of packs?</li><li>Can the supplier retrieve the records for a given serial number?</li></ol>

